WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.399
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.399 --> 00:00:08.425
Hi, I'm Szu-chi Huang.

00:00:08.425 --> 00:00:10.116
I'm the Associate Professor of

00:00:10.116 --> 00:00:10.805
Marketing at

00:00:10.805 --> 00:00:12.314
Stanford Business School.

00:00:12.314 --> 00:00:14.565
Before my academic career,

00:00:14.565 --> 00:00:17.472
I worked at JWT advertising agency,

00:00:17.472 --> 00:00:20.020
managing Asia Pacific market.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:22.180
I've taught many courses at

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:24.880
Stanford and the topic that excites

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:27.580
me the most is behavioral science,

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:29.020
the human factor.

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:31.212
There's so much to learn about how

00:00:31.212 --> 00:00:32.800
humans make decisions,

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.767
how humans get motivated, and

00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:36.896
how humans connect with others.

00:00:36.896 --> 00:00:38.860
I have five key takeaways from

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:41.061
the human factor to share with you

00:00:41.061 --> 00:00:41.618
today.

00:00:41.618 --> 00:00:47.647
[MUSIC]

00:00:47.647 --> 00:00:49.939
As leaders, we tend to rely on data

00:00:49.939 --> 00:00:51.520
for our decisions, and

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:53.575
we think that other people also

00:00:53.575 --> 00:00:55.962
will be motivated by the same data.

00:00:55.962 --> 00:00:57.909
As it turns out, most of our

00:00:57.909 --> 00:01:00.634
stakeholders are drawn to examples.

00:01:00.634 --> 00:01:02.733
Salient, top of my examples,

00:01:02.733 --> 00:01:05.077
become the guiding principles for

00:01:05.077 --> 00:01:06.788
their decision making.

00:01:06.788 --> 00:01:08.500
That is why people buy lottery

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.418
tickets, even though the actual

00:01:10.418 --> 00:01:12.475
probability of winning the lottery

00:01:12.475 --> 00:01:13.583
is extremely low.

00:01:13.583 --> 00:01:14.948
We were made to remember

00:01:14.948 --> 00:01:16.725
the examples of people winning

00:01:16.725 --> 00:01:18.364
the lottery much more so than

00:01:18.364 --> 00:01:20.279
thinking about the overwhelming

00:01:20.279 --> 00:01:22.350
examples of people who don't win.

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:24.281
So brainstorm ways to make your

00:01:24.281 --> 00:01:26.512
company's messages the lottery in

00:01:26.512 --> 00:01:28.156
your stakeholder's mind

00:01:28.156 --> 00:01:32.571
[MUSIC]

00:01:32.571 --> 00:01:34.103
As rational thinkers,

00:01:34.103 --> 00:01:35.020
we see pros and

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:36.474
cons in everything and

00:01:36.474 --> 00:01:38.625
can easily find the best choice.

00:01:38.625 --> 00:01:40.551
But for many other people,

00:01:40.551 --> 00:01:43.070
these pros and cons are ambiguous.

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:45.348
Therefore, every time they choose,

00:01:45.348 --> 00:01:47.634
they feel that they're losing some.

00:01:47.634 --> 00:01:49.775
Decisions are hard for them and

00:01:49.775 --> 00:01:51.840
there's always a trade off.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:53.927
So create a win-win option in

00:01:53.927 --> 00:01:56.597
the set, leverage default selection

00:01:56.597 --> 00:01:58.682
and think about how reference

00:01:58.682 --> 00:02:00.101
points can be used to

00:02:00.101 --> 00:02:01.863
make the choice easier.

00:02:01.863 --> 00:02:03.809
These elements helped to resolve

00:02:03.809 --> 00:02:05.199
decision difficulty,

00:02:05.199 --> 00:02:06.727
and make it easier for your

00:02:06.727 --> 00:02:08.962
stakeholders to come to conclusion.

00:02:08.962 --> 00:02:12.539
[MUSIC]

00:02:12.539 --> 00:02:14.248
Recent research has shown

00:02:14.248 --> 00:02:16.035
that while star ratings can

00:02:16.035 --> 00:02:18.054
predict the success of books,

00:02:18.054 --> 00:02:20.628
blockbuster movies and restaurants.

00:02:20.628 --> 00:02:23.065
It is the emotionality underlying

00:02:23.065 --> 00:02:25.501
the reviews that explain the sales

00:02:25.501 --> 00:02:26.262
results.

00:02:26.262 --> 00:02:28.594
In other words, not all five star

00:02:28.594 --> 00:02:30.182
ratings are the same.

00:02:30.182 --> 00:02:32.237
The ones with higher positive

00:02:32.237 --> 00:02:34.046
emotionality will lead to

00:02:34.046 --> 00:02:36.594
greater success and vice versa, for

00:02:36.594 --> 00:02:38.979
reviews that have strong negative

00:02:38.979 --> 00:02:40.635
emotionality in them.

00:02:40.635 --> 00:02:43.465
Therefore infuse your messages with

00:02:43.465 --> 00:02:44.289
emotion.

00:02:44.289 --> 00:02:46.166
The more emotional people can

00:02:46.166 --> 00:02:47.817
get about your product and

00:02:47.817 --> 00:02:49.769
your company, the more likely

00:02:49.769 --> 00:02:51.645
they will be motivated to stay

00:02:51.645 --> 00:02:53.685
with you through ups and downs.

00:02:53.685 --> 00:02:57.630
[MUSIC]

00:02:57.630 --> 00:02:59.592
We not now think about how we

00:02:59.592 --> 00:03:02.152
personally feel about a decision,

00:03:02.152 --> 00:03:04.540
we also care a lot about how others

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:06.774
think about these decisions.

00:03:06.774 --> 00:03:08.883
Social influences are extremely

00:03:08.883 --> 00:03:10.765
powerful and that is why we buy

00:03:10.765 --> 00:03:13.032
products to keep up with our peers.

00:03:13.032 --> 00:03:15.632
We follow so-called experts advice,

00:03:15.632 --> 00:03:17.285
and we watch TV shows that

00:03:17.285 --> 00:03:19.490
are rated well by others even when

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:21.710
these others are strangers too.

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:23.445
Successful leaders know how

00:03:23.445 --> 00:03:25.179
to leverage social forces so

00:03:25.179 --> 00:03:27.058
that people can be confident in

00:03:27.058 --> 00:03:29.010
their decisions and even become

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:30.753
ambassadors for the brand.

00:03:30.753 --> 00:03:32.715
It is good to know that a decision

00:03:32.715 --> 00:03:34.677
is accurate, but it is even more

00:03:34.677 --> 00:03:36.860
important to know that others think

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:38.841
that the decision is accurate.

00:03:38.841 --> 00:03:42.162
[MUSIC]

00:03:42.162 --> 00:03:44.566
Humans do not live in a vacuum,

00:03:44.566 --> 00:03:46.700
there is always a context.

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:48.736
The context of our environment

00:03:48.736 --> 00:03:50.930
activates important goals which

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:52.425
shape our behaviors.

00:03:52.425 --> 00:03:54.642
By taking people from one context

00:03:54.642 --> 00:03:57.096
to another, we can therefore change

00:03:57.096 --> 00:03:59.563
the key drivers of their decisions.

00:03:59.563 --> 00:04:01.665
The context can be, one, the

00:04:01.665 --> 00:04:04.276
physical environment such as moving

00:04:04.276 --> 00:04:06.477
from your office to your home.

00:04:06.477 --> 00:04:09.465
Second, the cultural surroundings,

00:04:09.465 --> 00:04:11.999
like how many of us have moved from

00:04:11.999 --> 00:04:13.910
one country to another?

00:04:13.910 --> 00:04:17.827
Three, a social context and four,

00:04:17.827 --> 00:04:20.184
a temporal context.

00:04:20.184 --> 00:04:22.110
My research has shown that sales

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:24.035
team behave very differently in

00:04:24.035 --> 00:04:26.175
the beginning of the month compared

00:04:26.175 --> 00:04:28.099
to the end of the month as they get

00:04:28.099 --> 00:04:29.899
closer to their sales goals.

00:04:29.899 --> 00:04:32.195
That is a powerful change in their

00:04:32.195 --> 00:04:33.593
temporal context.

00:04:33.593 --> 00:04:35.345
Staying static will make you

00:04:35.345 --> 00:04:36.296
ineffective.

00:04:36.296 --> 00:04:38.250
Successful leaders are nimble

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:40.053
because they understand how

00:04:40.053 --> 00:04:41.555
critical context is and

00:04:41.555 --> 00:04:43.066
how fast it can change.

00:04:43.066 --> 00:04:45.364
We can all become more effective

00:04:45.364 --> 00:04:47.498
leaders and communicators.

00:04:47.498 --> 00:04:49.760
The knowledge of the human factor

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:51.132
can be learned, and

00:04:51.132 --> 00:04:53.394
with practice we become better at

00:04:53.394 --> 00:04:55.185
leveraging these ideas.

00:04:55.185 --> 00:04:56.889
When I moved from Taiwan to

00:04:56.889 --> 00:04:58.438
the United States, and

00:04:58.438 --> 00:05:00.298
from industry to academia,

00:05:00.298 --> 00:05:02.079
I realized that the salient

00:05:02.079 --> 00:05:04.103
examples are different here.

00:05:04.103 --> 00:05:05.968
What people get emotional about

00:05:05.968 --> 00:05:06.935
is different and

00:05:06.935 --> 00:05:09.008
the social elements and the context

00:05:09.008 --> 00:05:10.883
are all completely different.

00:05:10.883 --> 00:05:12.755
So I had to relearn how

00:05:12.755 --> 00:05:15.460
I communicate, how I teach and

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:16.506
how I lead.

00:05:16.506 --> 00:05:18.692
It is certainly a challenge, but

00:05:18.692 --> 00:05:20.564
it has also been a very fun and

00:05:20.564 --> 00:05:21.896
fulfilling journey

00:05:21.896 --> 00:05:31.896
[MUSIC]

